# Feature evaluators

Enable the optional `feature` component in the owning App and run `forj generate --feature` after declaring evaluator resources in `.env`. The native memory and file drivers are always compiled, following the framework baseline-driver convention. `FEATURE_SUPPORTED_DRIVERS=memory,file` records their build capability; `FEATURE_DRIVER` selects the active native source at runtime. Vendor SDK adapters are outside this first integration.

The default memory source captures `FEATURE_DOCUMENT` at startup, using an empty document when omitted. File sources require `FEATURE_PATH` and load synchronously before startup. Set `FEATURE_REFRESH_INTERVAL` (at least `10ms`, default `30s`) and `FEATURE_INITIALIZATION_TIMEOUT` (positive, default `5s`). The App lifecycle starts refresh and cancels and joins workers on shutdown. Invalid refresh retains valid decisions and keeps readiness usable. `Instances()` exposes the last refresh error and freshness timestamps; startup still fails when no valid initial configuration can be loaded.

A named evaluator uses `FEATURE_CHECKOUT_DRIVER=file` and `FEATURE_CHECKOUT_PATH=storage/flags/checkout.json`. Regenerate to add `Manager.Checkout()`.

Create `storage/flags/checkout.json` before starting the App. This relative path assumes the process runs from the Project root; relative paths resolve from the process working directory. A deployment can configure an absolute path for a mounted definition file.

Named Apps use their existing environment overlay, for example `BILLING_FEATURE_CHECKOUT_PATH`. Resources identify evaluator configuration, not individual flag names. A name declared only for Billing is not initialized by another App. Accessing an undeclared App resource fails fast.

Inject `*features.Manager` into an application service through the owning App's service Wire set. Capture one request snapshot before targeting:

```go
snapshot, err := manager.Checkout().Snapshot()
if err != nil {
    return err
}
request := snapshot.For(feature.Target{Key: tenantID})
checkout := request.Evaluate(ctx, "checkout-v2", false)
receipt := request.Evaluate(ctx, "receipt-v2", false)
```

The ordinary `For` targeting scope does not freeze provider configuration. A snapshot captures both definitions and evaluation time. Dynamic flag names and tenant rollout rules stay in the source document. Keep authorization separate from presentation decisions.
